  {
    "episode_id": 90083,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90083.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "It's really exciting. And you're seeing this state come into its own. And this is a state, as you probably know, that's been voter suppressed and voter intimidated. effectively for the last 144 years and it's produced one of the worst voter turnout states in the union and not because we love democracy less than the folks in California or New York or Idaho or anywhere else. It's just we were drawn that way. Racial gerrymandering that diminishes the power of black voters and Latino voters and it really undermines the likelihood that they're going to vote at all The most onerous voter ID laws in the country, 750 polling place closures over the last eight years, and it'll be no surprise to you that most of those polling place closures occurred in the cities with the fastest growing black and Latino populations. And in this very cynical approach to 2020, where in the midst of this pandemic, for a virus against which none of us have immunity. They are not allowing mail-in voting except in very extraordinary circumstances. So they close all these polling places and they're concentrating more people at fewer locations and forcing them to vote in person. So this is what we're up against. But because of the work that so many volunteers are doing, over the last four years, since the last presidential cycle, more than 2.1 million new voters have entered the roles in Texas. I think that may be more than the population of many states in the union. And that's just the new voter since 2016. And as you can guess, those voter registrations tend to favor Democrats as this state becomes increasingly diverse, a minority majority state, and is home to the most diverse city in the country. It's used in Texas where they speak 120 languages in the public schools. I believe against all those obstacles that I laid out just now, that the diversity, the strength, and the power of people that you're finding in Texas is going to overcome these challenges and produce a record turnout. As we did in 2018, a record turnout in this presidential election year that will help to elect a democratic majority to the State House, which can then begin to dismantle this infrastructure of suppression that has been institutionalized forever in the State of Texas. And in this, and this is the holy grail, I think in American politics, the 38 electoral college votes in Texas. If they are pledged to the Democratic nominee in Joe Biden for the first time in 44 years, 1976 was the last time Texas voted for a Democrat for president. If that happens, I think the impact is going to be seismic. It will absolutely shock the conscience of this country. And whether or not Donald Trump accepts the results in the national election I think the nation will be forced to because Texas is indisputable. You know the game is over when you've lost Texas and it forever reorders the political landscape and what is possible for the foreseeable future. So this, all our marbles are in Texas on this one and it is totally possible by the numbers, by the mass, by the trajectory we've been on and these powered by people volunteers have made 25 million voter contact attempts over the last six months. And we hope to do another 10 to 15 before voting begins on October 13th, really soon, I think 33, 34 days away. So yes, a lot on the line, but I feel very, very good about our prospects here in Texas.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
